{ignore}
<p><code>{true}</code> answers TRUE - the plain hit of a level. Written as a pair, its content
renders exactly once and there is no data to iterate; written alone it prints nothing. It is
the fixed "yes" of the three constant tags, beside <a href="?tag/false">{false}</a> and
<a href="?tag/null">{null}</a>.</p>

<p>Its use is as a neutral level to hang options on. Every tag takes the generic options, so
<code>{true tidy}...{/true}</code> tidies a stretch of HTML, <code>{true toContent='box'}</code>
keeps its rendered content in a store, and <code>{true $total = 0}...{/true}</code> gives a
block a variable of its own that is unwound when the block closes. In tests it switches a
branch on without changing anything else.</p>

<p>Because it is a hit, an <code>@else@</code> part of a <code>{true}</code> never renders.</p>
{/ignore}
